,"tokio-rustls","; terminate TLS and forward plain TCP.",[29,907,908,911],{},[32,909,910],{},"Bittorrent client",": piece assembly, peer protocol, async I\u002FO.",[29,913,914,738,917,920,921,924,925,928],{},[32,915,916],{},"Operating system kernel",[61,918,919],{},"no_std","; ",[61,922,923],{}," bootloader","; serial driver; minimal shell. (",[61,926,927],{},"blog_os"," tutorial.)",[29,930,931,934],{},[32,932,933],{},"Database engine",": B+tree storage, WAL, MVCC. Hard but illuminating.",[29,936,937,940,941,944],{},[32,938,939],{},"Compiler",": parse a small language; lower to LLVM IR via ",[61,942,943],{},"inkwell"," or to Cranelift.",[29,946,947,950],{},[32,948,949],{},"Static site generator",": markdown → HTML, templates, RSS, syntax highlighting.",[29,952,953,956,957,356,960,963],{},[32,954,955],{},"WASM image editor",": client-side, ",[61,958,959],{},"wasm-bindgen",[61,961,962],{},"Canvas"," API.",[29,965,966,969,970,972,973,64],{},[32,967,968],{},"Realtime multiplayer game server",": WebSocket + ",[61,971,772],{},"; per-room state with ",[61,974,822],{},[18,976,978],{"id":977},"reading-code-to-mastery","Reading Code to Mastery",[53,980,982],{"id":981},"why-reading-source-accelerates-learning","Why reading source accelerates learning",[14,984,985,986,989,990,993,994,997,998,1001,1002,1005,1006,1009,1010,1013,1014,1017,1018,1021],{},"Reading high-quality Rust source ",[32,987,988],{},"internalizes idioms"," you can't get from tutorials: how real crates structure error types, where they draw module boundaries, how they use traits for extensibility, how they handle ",[61,991,992],{},"unsafe"," soundness. The standard library especially is ",[32,995,996],{},"heavily reviewed, performance-tuned Rust"," — reading ",[61,999,1000],{},"alloc::vec"," teaches you the ownership\u002Fmove patterns and amortized-allocation reasoning that ",[61,1003,1004],{},"Vec"," embodies. ",[32,1007,1008],{},"How to approach a large crate",": start with the public API (",[61,1011,1012],{},"src\u002Flib.rs","'s ",[61,1015,1016],{},"pub"," items), trace one common call path end-to-end, then read the internals only once you understand what the API promises. ",[32,1019,1020],{},"When to do this",": once you're past the basics (chapters 1-20) and want to bridge from \"can write Rust\" to \"can read any Rust\" — reading source is the bridge to working on real codebases.",[14,1023,1024],{},"Read source of:",[26,1026,1027,1033,1038,1047,1055,1062,1067,1073,1078],{},[29,1028,1029,1032],{},[61,1030,1031],{},"std"," (slice\u002Fiter\u002Fvec modules).",[29,1034,1035,1037],{},[61,1036,766],{}," (scheduler, channels).",[29,1039,1040,1042,1043,1046],{},[61,1041,757],{}," (derive macros — ",[61,1044,1045],{},"serde_derive",").",[29,1048,1049,1051,1052,1046],{},[61,1050,772],{}," (routing, middleware via ",[61,1053,1054],{},"tower",[29,1056,1057,1059,1060,1046],{},[61,1058,842],{}," (HTTP client on ",[61,1061,769],{},[29,1063,1064,1066],{},[61,1065,870],{}," (compile-time SQL checking via macros).",[29,1068,1069,1072],{},[61,1070,1071],{},"crossbeam"," (epoch-based memory reclamation).",[29,1074,1075,1077],{},[61,1076,895],{}," (ECS, scheduling, renderer).",[29,1079,1080,1083],{},[61,1081,1082],{},"regex"," (DFA construction, Unicode tables).",[14,1085,1086,1087,356,1089,356,1092,64],{},"The standard library is the best Rust code you can read. Start with ",[61,1088,1000],{},[61,1090,1091],{},"core::iter",[61,1093,1094],{},"core::slice",[18,1096,1098],{"id":1097},"practice-sites","Practice Sites",[26,1100,1101,1107,1113,1119,1125],{},[29,1102,1103,1106],{},[32,1104,1105],{},"Rustlings",": small exercises for each concept.",[29,1108,1109,1112],{},[32,1110,1111],{},"Exercism Rust track",": mentor-reviewed exercises.",[29,1114,1115,1118],{},[32,1116,1117],{},"Advent of Code",": annual puzzles, perfect for Rust.",[29,1120,1121,1124],{},[32,1122,1123],{},"LeetCode Rust",": algos.",[29,1126,1127,1130],{},[32,1128,1129],{},"Rosetta Code Rust",": idiomatic translations.",[18,1132,1134],{"id":1133},"open-source-contribution","Open Source Contribution",[26,1136,1137,1143,1149,1155,1161],{},[29,1138,1139,1142],{},[61,1140,1141],{},"rust-lang\u002Frust",": good-first-issue labels; tough but rewarding.",[29,1144,1145,1148],{},[61,1146,1147],{},"tokio-rs\u002F*",": tokio ecosystem.",[29,1150,1151,1154],{},[61,1152,1153],{},"serde-rs\u002F*",": serde, serde_json.",[29,1156,1157,1160],{},[61,1158,1159],{},"bevyengine\u002Fbevy",": game engine.",[29,1162,1163,1166],{},[61,1164,1165],{},"rust-cli\u002F*",": CLI tool templates.",[18,1168,1170],{"id":1169},"mastery-self-check","Mastery Self-Check",[14,1172,1173],{},"Can you confidently:",[26,1175,1176,1179,1190,1197,1207,1216,1225,1261,1275,1282,1295],{},[29,1177,1178],{},"Explain ownership without using the word \"borrow\"? (Use \"one owner, moved on assignment, dropped on scope-end\".)",[29,1180,1181,1182,1185,1186,1189],{},"Predict whether ",[61,1183,1184],{},"&'static"," is required for a thread closure? (Yes if it borrows stack data — use ",[61,1187,1188],{},"move",".)",[29,1191,1192,1193,1196],{},"Read ",[61,1194,1195],{},"for\u003C'a> Fn(&'a str) -> &'a str"," and explain it? (HRTB; the closure works for any lifetime.)",[29,1198,1199,1200,326,1203,1206],{},"Implement a trait for both ",[61,1201,1202],{},"&T",[61,1204,1205],{},"&mut T","? (Avoid; usually one suffices.)",[29,1208,1209,1210,1212,1213,1189],{},"Implement ",[61,1211,432],{}," for your own type? (Just ",[61,1214,1215],{},"next()",[29,1217,1218,1219,1221,1222,1224],{},"Explain why ",[61,1220,1205],{}," is invariant in ",[61,1223,100],{},"? (Otherwise you could swap in shorter-lived data.)",[29,1226,1227,1228,356,1231,356,1234,356,1237,356,1240,1243,1244,1246,1247,920,1249,1252,1253,1255,1256,1258,1259,1189],{},"Choose between ",[61,1229,1230],{},"Rc",[61,1232,1233],{},"Arc",[61,1235,1236],{},"Cell",[61,1238,1239],{},"RefCell",[61,1241,1242],{},"Mutex","? (Single-thread shared\u002Fclone: ",[61,1245,1230],{},"; multi-thread: ",[61,1248,1233],{},[61,1250,1251],{},"Copy"," interior: ",[61,1254,1236],{},"; mut interior single-thread: ",[61,1257,1239],{},"; mut interior multi-thread: ",[61,1260,1242],{},[29,1262,1263,1264,1267,1268,1271,1272,1274],{},"Read a ",[61,1265,1266],{},"Pin\u003C&mut T>"," and know what ",[61,1269,1270],{},"!Unpin"," implies? (Can't safely move the ",[61,1273,100],{}," after pinning.)",[29,1276,1277,1278,1281],{},"Reason about variance of your custom smart pointer? (Pick ",[61,1279,1280],{},"PhantomData"," accordingly.)",[29,1283,1284,1285,1288,1289,330,1292,1189],{},"Write a ",[61,1286,1287],{},"proc_macro_derive","? (Use ",[61,1290,1291],{},"syn",[61,1293,1294],{},"quote",[29,1296,1192,1297,1300],{},[61,1298,1299],{},"unsafe { *ptr }"," and verify the safety invariants? (Alignment, initialization, aliasing, lifetime.)",[14,1302,1303],{},"If you can do all of the above without consulting docs, you're a pro Rust developer.",[18,1305,1307],{"id":1306},"tips-tricks","💡 Tips & Tricks",[26,1309,1310,1326,1336,1347,1358,1371],{},[29,1311,1312,1315,1316,1319,1320,330,1323,1325],{},[32,1313,1314],{},"Idiom",": implement the beginner exercises twice — once reaching for ",[61,1317,1318],{},"unwrap()","\u002Fsimple loops to get something working, then a second pass replacing panics with ",[61,1321,1322],{},"Result",[61,1324,325],{}," and loops with iterator chains. The diff between your two versions is the fastest way to internalize idiomatic Rust.",[29,1327,1328,1331,1332,1335],{},[32,1329,1330],{},"Debug",": for the harder exercises (lock-free queue, custom allocator, FFI wrapper), run ",[61,1333,1334],{},"cargo miri test"," on your solution before trusting it — these are exactly the categories of code where \"it compiles and passes my tests\" and \"it's actually sound\" can diverge.",[29,1337,1338,1340,1341,356,1343,1346],{},[32,1339,1314],{},": for the linked-list exercise specifically, resist the urge to look up \"why is a linked list hard in Rust\" until you've hit the ownership wall yourself — the struggle with ",[61,1342,351],{},[61,1344,1345],{},"Option",", and mutable iteration is the actual lesson, not the working code at the end.",[29,1348,1349,738,1351,1354,1355,1357],{},[32,1350,1330],{},[61,1352,1353],{},"cargo expand"," on your own ",[61,1356,1287],{}," attempts shows you exactly what your macro generated — indispensable when the derive \"does nothing\" or produces a confusing downstream error far from the macro definition.",[29,1359,1360,1363,1364,1366,1367,1370],{},[32,1361,1362],{},"Performance",": for the lock-free queue and custom allocator exercises, benchmark against the crate you're imitating (",[61,1365,658],{},", the system allocator) with ",[61,1368,1369],{},"criterion"," — matching correctness is the first milestone, matching performance is a much higher bar and worth measuring honestly rather than assuming.",[29,1372,1373,1375,1376,373,1378,1380],{},[32,1374,1314],{},": when stuck on a project for more than 30 minutes, read relevant ",[61,1377,1031],{},[61,1379,766],{}," source before searching for a tutorial — the self-check list in this chapter exists because reading real, production-grade Rust is how the concepts from earlier chapters actually cement.",[18,1382,1384],{"id":1383},"final-words","Final Words",[14,1386,1387],{},"Rust has a steep learning curve but pays dividends. The compiler is strict but kind: errors are messages, not crashes. Once you internalize ownership, lifetimes, and the trait system, the rest is vocabulary.",[14,1389,1390,1391,1393,1394,1396,1397,1400,1401,1404],{},"Build things. Break things. Read the stdlib. Read ",[61,1392,766],{},". Run ",[61,1395,1353],{}," on macros. Run ",[61,1398,1399],{},"Miri"," on unsafe. Profile with ",[61,1402,1403],{},"samply",". Contribute to open source.",[14,1406,1407],{},"Welcome to being a Rust developer.",[14,1409,1410],{},"🦀",[1412,1413,1414],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#